  "LEZ-24-120|C|2026-11-19": {"short":"Part swap: STS-M12 150 A master contactor","change":"Source audit (2026-11-19): K1 is named, a Sky-Tec STS-M12 (Lamar STS-A12 class) at 150 A continuous / 300 A inrush, against the ≥ 100 A requirement (the common White-Rodgers Type 70 is 80 A and does not qualify); mass 0.92 lb (was 0.60); elec-k now governed by K2 at +0.36; cost $160."},
  "LEZ-24-130|A|2026-11-19": {"short":"Initial definition","change":"Initial definition. Block J listed 17 protected circuits against B's 6-breaker cut-out (J-F17). B's LEZ-31-100 cut-out is kept unchanged: the six circuits a pilot may need to pull or reset in flight (alternator field, landing brake, strobes, AXIS COM, transponder, boost pump) are push-pull breakers on a sub-panel behind it; the other 12 are ATC blade fuses in two fuse blocks (main FB1, essential FB2) on a G-10 module on the left side wall forward of the panel, with the main bus bar, the essential feed diode D1, the bus-end ANL F2 and the cabin ground block GB1. AXIS A is on the main bus, AXIS B on the essential bus. The old \"MAG 1A\" fuse is the GMU 11 feed."},
  "LEZ-24-130|B|2026-11-19": {"short":"AXIS A on the essential bus, B on the main (J's ask); feeds checked","change":"Final integration: block J asked for the AXIS GDU 116C's power input A on the essential bus and B on the main bus; the two fuse positions swap their labels (FB2:1 AXIS A, FB1:1 AXIS B) and wires P21A18 / P20A18 land on J1:1 / J1:2 (no wire or length change). I6 traces every consumer's supply to its protector; I7 records block B as host only."},
  "LEZ-24-130|C|2026-11-19": {"short":"Klixon 7277-2, Ø 0.445 holes","change":"Source audit (2026-11-19): the breakers are Klixon 7277-2 (MS3320 was a wrong cross-reference: the MIL part is MS26574); their panel hole is Ø 0.445 plus a 0.125 anti-rotation hole (was a 3/8 bushing cut): the bushings still fall inside B's cut-out (I1); breaker mass 0.062 lb (was 0.055); cost about $300."},
  "LEZ-24-130|D|2026-11-19": {"short":"K3 feed check","change":"I7 reads the start relay K3's contact feed on FB1-6 (the source audit moved it there; the check still expected FB1-4)."},
  "LEZ-24-140|A|2026-11-19": {"short":"Initial definition","change":"Initial definition: a single-point (star) ground at the battery − post (ICD G-O6). GB1 on the bus module collects every cabin return and goes to BT1 − by N3A8; GB2 on the firewall (A's layout holes) collects the firewall-aft returns, the key switch's P-lead ground and the engine strap, and goes to BT1 − by the 2 AWG ground cable. The engine case is grounded only through LEZ-24-165 to E1 on the accessory housing."},
  "LEZ-24-140|B|2026-11-19": {"short":"B&C GB24, 5/16 stud","change":"Source audit (2026-11-19): GB1 named as the B&C GB24 (24 tabs, 5/16 in stud; no 20-tab block is listed): the stud is 5/16 in (was 1/4). The GB24's 1-3/8 × 3-1/2 in footprint is larger than the drawn envelope: the module re-layout is open (docs/blocks/i_electrical.md). Cost $130 for two."},
  "LEZ-24-150|A|2026-11-19": {"short":"Initial definition","change":"Initial definition. VR1 moves to A's 'ovr' layout holes on the firewall aft face (FS 153, BL 0.60 / 2.00), clear of the mount brackets' keep-outs. The 40 A alternator is part of the engine (block G LEZ-71-210); its B+ lead is LEZ-24-160."},
  "LEZ-24-150|B|2026-11-19": {"short":"B&C LR3E-14 named, 0.56 lb","change":"Source audit (2026-11-19): VR1 is the B&C LR3E-14 (07-06742): 0.56 lb (was 0.30), crowbar 16.25 V, field < 4 A with a 5 A field breaker; the field load is now the 4.0 A conservative end (elec-coord ALT FIELD 5 A against 1.25 × 4.0 A: MS 0.00). Cost $385."},
  "LEZ-24-160|A|2026-11-19": {"short":"Initial definition","change":"Initial definition. The cabin run leaves the bus module under the panel's bottom edge (no panel hole), joins raceway LP (now WL 35.04), leaves it at FS 124 through the rear seat bulkhead, passes the left sump above F's feed, under the CS spar into its boot lane; aft of the firewall the B-lead and field leads climb between K2 and VR1, cross the crankcase under the HT leads and drop between the right front cylinder and the ring gear to the alternator (G-O4 clearances, fire-sleeved). The boost-pump lead runs at 2.5 A to F's pump."},
  "LEZ-24-160|B|2026-11-19": {"short":"Tinned M22759/16 data","change":"Source audit (2026-11-19): wire resistance, mass and OD are the AS22759/16 tin-plated maxima (Glenair data sheet): resistance +0 to +27 %, mass −18 to −39 %; protector chart AC 43.13-1B Table 11-3 confirmed; bundle derating from the Fig. 11-5 table (floor 0.5075)."},
  "LEZ-24-160|C|2026-11-19": {"short":"Under the panel edge","change":"The cabin heads rise aft of FS 44.5 (was 43.0), so both paths stay ≥ 1/8 in under the panel's bottom cut through its whole thickness (they grazed it by up to 0.22 in)."},
  "LEZ-24-160|D|2026-11-22": {"short":"Re-routed past the moved selector knob","change":"The left power raceway re-steps along the wall past the fuel selector knob's new position (LEZ-28-135, seat moved 6 in forward).","phase":"seat-1"},
  "LEZ-24-161|A|2026-11-19": {"short":"Initial definition","change":"Initial definition. The trunk runs at FS 36.9, WL 40.3, under the edge of J's avionics shelf and forward of the AXIS connectors, with the right riser inboard of the pitch rod and under J's right static line; the S12 lead leaves the RH NG-30 plate through E's grommet and is wired C–NC (alarm closed with the gear not down), S13 likewise from latch hook 1 through raceway LS, S11 C–NO (closed at idle)."},
